.TH LSUSD 1 "2026-08-21" "lsusd 2.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsusd \- list USB devices with their associated USB metadata
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsusd
.RB [ \-p | \-c | \-j ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-\-hubs ]
.RB [ \-\-serial ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.br
.B lsusd
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsusd
discovers USB devices and displays their associated USB metadata
(bus, address, vendor, product, serial number, VID:PID, device release, speed)
in a formatted table. It is intended as a zero-dependency
.BR lsusb (8)
successor for macOS and Linux.
.PP
By default,
.B lsusd
lists all non-hub USB devices. USB hubs are hidden unless
.BR \-\-hubs
is supplied.
.PP
On macOS, USB device discovery is performed via the
.I IOUSB
registry plane exposed by
.BR ioreg (8),
avoiding the stale
.I SPUSBDataType
system_profiler data type used by older macOS
.B lsusb
wrappers.
.PP
On Linux, USB device discovery is performed via
.I /sys/bus/usb/devices .
Serial mode checks every tty device for a USB ancestor, so devices renamed via
udev rules are also detected.
.PP
With
.BR \-w " / " \-\-watch ,
.B lsusd
first prints currently connected USB serial devices as
.I present
events in a live terminal table, then waits for USB serial device changes and
redraws the table. This mode is event-driven: on macOS it uses IOKit serial
service notifications, and on Linux it listens to kernel uevents through the
netlink socket. A fresh snapshot is taken only after an event arrives, in order
to compute add/remove rows.
.PP
With
.BR \-a " / " \-\-all ,
.B lsusd
uses the default USB device mode explicitly. This flag is kept for
compatibility with scripts and cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-serial
or
.BR \-w .
.PP
With
.BR \-\-serial ,
.B lsusd
shows only USB serial device nodes and their USB metadata.
.PP
With
.BR \-t " / " \-\-tree ,
.B lsusd
shows the USB device hierarchy. Like
.BR \-\-all ,
this hides hubs by default and includes them with
.BR \-\-hubs .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plain
Plain tab\-separated output without headers or box drawing.
Suitable for consumption by
.BR cut (1),
.BR awk (1),
or shell scripts.
The columns match the active output mode.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-csv
CSV output with a header row.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
JSON array output.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-spinner
Disable the progress spinner shown during device discovery.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Show all non-hub USB devices. This is the default mode; the flag is kept for
compatibility and explicitness.
The columns are: bus, address, location, product, vendor, serial, vidpid,
release, speed.
.PP
The release field is the BCD-coded
.I bcdDevice
value from the standard USB device descriptor. Manufacturers commonly use it
for the firmware release, but USB defines it more generally as a
manufacturer-assigned device release number. It uses the conventional USB
two-group notation; for example, 0x0100 is displayed as 1.00. No
major/minor/patch interpretation is inferred. A missing value is displayed as
.IR ? .
.TP
.B \-\-serial
Show only USB serial device nodes.
The columns are: device, product, vendor, serial, vidpid, release.
.TP
.B \-\-hubs
Include USB hubs in
.BR \-\-all
and
.BR \-\-tree
output.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tree
Show USB devices as a tree.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-watch
Show current USB serial devices in a live table, then watch changes until
interrupted.
With
.BR \-j ,
events are emitted as newline-delimited JSON objects. With
.BR \-c ,
the columns are: action, device, product, vendor, serial, vidpid, release. With
.BR \-p ,
the same columns are emitted as tab-separated text without a header.
The action is
.IR present ,
.IR add ,
or
.IR remove .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (devices found or no devices found).
.TP
.B 1
Unsupported platform.
.SH EXAMPLES
List all non-hub USB devices in the default table format:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd
.RE
.PP
Get plain output for scripting:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-p | cut \-f1
.RE
.PP
Export as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-j > devices.json
.RE
.PP
List all non-hub USB devices explicitly:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-\-all
.RE
.PP
List all USB devices including hubs:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-\-all \-\-hubs
.RE
.PP
Show the USB hierarchy:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-\-tree
.RE
.PP
List USB serial device nodes:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-\-serial
.RE
.PP
Watch for USB serial add/remove events:
.PP
.RS
.B lsusd \-\-watch
.RE
.SH AUTHORS
Dr.\& Michael Lauer
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioreg (8),
.BR lsusb (8)
